1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
They can rip through armor and light up the sky, travel at speeds up to 3,000 miles an hour.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:14,000
They can be designed to kill or just disable.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Some of them seem to think for themselves.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Bullets on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,440
After 9-11, Americans realized they were vulnerable.

6
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,720
Security was dramatically tightened,

7
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:45,780
particularly at airports and on planes.

8
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,220
Air marshals armed with handguns were assigned to flights,

9
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:53,460
and some pilots began carrying pistols in the cockpit.

10
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:58,480
Suddenly, a blazing shootout with hijackers at 30,000 feet

11
00:00:58,481 --> 00:01:02,920
in a pressurized passenger cabin was a real possibility.

12
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:10,220
There's a popular belief that a single bullet hole through an airplane's fuselage

13
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:15,160
will cause a depressurization so violent that the plane will break up in midair.

14
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:18,740
That is simply not true.

15
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:24,940
What does concern aviation safety experts are stray bullets hitting passengers.

16
00:01:24,941 --> 00:01:33,480
There is a special kind of bullet that substantially reduces the chance of a ricocheting shot hitting an innocent passenger.

17
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:35,740
It's called the frangible.

18
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:47,220
Unlike conventional bullets that remain intact, frangibles penetrate flesh but break up into small, less harmful pieces upon contact with anything hard.

19
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:53,220
Conventional projectiles are made with a lead core and a copper jacket.

20
00:01:53,221 --> 00:01:53,921
jacket.

21
00:01:53,922 --> 00:02:00,600
Now we have centering and copper matrix technology and other metals that don't have to

22
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:01,459
use a jacket.

23
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:06,940
And so basically the bullet turns to dust when it hits a steel target or any really

24
00:02:06,941 --> 00:02:12,340
hard object. This has gone a long way to reducing the overpenetration problem.

25
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:18,740
Centerfire, the major maker of frangibles, says of its product,

26
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:24,440
Our bullet can penetrate a bad guy's head, but it won't come out the other side.

27
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,380
Frangibles are packed with finely ground copper and tin.

28
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,400
Specific manufacturing details are proprietary,

29
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:36,360
but the ground metals are combined either by high-pressure packing

30
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:40,240
or with a bonding agent, such as nylon or polyurethane.

31
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:45,460
Frangibles are being used increasingly by police and the military.

32
00:02:45,461 --> 00:02:50,360
The problems that we had with the standard ball was over-penetration.

33
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:54,940
Shooting in one room, exiting through another, taking out friendlies on the other side.

34
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:59,620
So what we came up with to prevent this from happening was a frangible round,

35
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:01,220
designed to fragment on contact.

36
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:06,000
So we have Marines training room to room with live ammunition.

37
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:10,540
Many police shootings occur at close range.

38
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,340
In some cases, the target is no further away than the end of the muzzle.

39
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:23,460
Their bullets can pass through the target and still have the force to hit a wall and bounce back at the shooter with deadly effect.

40
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:27,860
Police officers have been killed and wounded by their own shots.

41
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:31,920
A frangible will break up before it can bounce back.

42
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:42,820
But the focus today with the frangible is protecting our own inside a close-quarter environment.

43
00:03:42,821 --> 00:03:49,820
Frangibles are just one member of an immense family of bullets with a huge range of different characteristics.

44
00:03:49,821 --> 00:03:59,820
Almost all bullets, though, are literally the tip of a much larger self-contained package.

45
00:03:59,821 --> 00:04:03,820
The bullet is the projectile part of a cartridge.

46
00:04:03,821 --> 00:04:09,820
Traditional bullets usually have lead cores, covered by a hard jacket of copper or brass.

47
00:04:09,821 --> 00:04:10,542
or brass.

48
00:04:10,543 --> 00:04:17,280
The bullet is gripped at its base by a brass casing, which also holds the propellant

49
00:04:17,281 --> 00:04:18,271
and the primer.

50
00:04:18,272 --> 00:04:22,960
The propellant inside the casing is smokeless powder, finely granulated

51
00:04:22,961 --> 00:04:23,949
nitrocellulose.

52
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:28,960
The copper or steel primer cup at the base of the casing holds an explosive,

53
00:04:28,961 --> 00:04:31,305
which is pressure sensitive.

54
00:04:31,306 --> 00:04:36,580
The fully assembled bullet and casing is a cartridge, or round.

55
00:04:36,581 --> 00:04:43,540
Whether used in a single-shot weapon or one fed by a magazine of ammunition,

56
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:47,740
each cartridge must be cycled through the weapon and fed into the chamber.

57
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:56,440
The chamber is where the combustion takes place.

58
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:01,680
When you pull the trigger, the firing pin hits the primer.

59
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:08,480
The primer contains a very small amount of explosive compound, which produces a flame.

60
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:15,800
The flame goes through a small hole in the base of a case and ignites the powder charge.

61
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:20,500
The powder charge burns. It does not explode. It burns.

62
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:28,280
And it moves the bullet out the barrel and also gives it a twist, usually a right-hand twist.

63
00:05:28,281 --> 00:05:31,840
and that twist is what stabilizes a bullet in flight.

64
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:35,420
Sounds simple.

65
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,080
Took about 500 years to figure out.

66
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,380
The development of the bullet was slow

67
00:05:42,381 --> 00:05:45,100
compared with the pace of improvement in the weapons that fired them.

68
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,320
The earliest projectiles would have been stones, rocks,

69
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,880
something that was thrown by hand first, the caveman,

70
00:05:55,881 --> 00:06:01,020
would have evolved from basically throwing stones to perhaps the anvil,

71
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,300
which was basically a spear projectile,

72
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:07,900
eventually moving into projectiles that were used oftentimes on shafts,

73
00:06:07,901 --> 00:06:14,000
that is as javelins, as arrows, as a spear, and also as hand arms, darts.

74
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:19,920
Around the 9th century, the Chinese invented gunpowder.

75
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:22,980
Firecrackers scared off demons.

76
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,760
Simple rockets provided delightful spectacles.

77
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,080
and were also used as primitive weapons.

78
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:33,700
The Chinese certainly learned that they could also project projectiles out of tubes.

79
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,480
Some of the first firearms were nothing more than a bamboo tube

80
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:38,460
with a polished rock and some gunpowder thrown at the bottom.

81
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,300
It took hundreds of years for the secret of gunpowder to make its way to Europe.

82
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:50,160
It wasn't until the 13th century that Europeans began experimenting with it and its uses.

83
00:06:50,161 --> 00:07:01,500
Soon after, they were producing the first handguns and the world's first real bullet, the round

84
00:07:01,501 --> 00:07:02,500
lead ball.

85
00:07:02,501 --> 00:07:07,000
The history of the round lead ball basically spans centuries.

86
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:11,560
Part of the reason for that is the round projectiles would have been easier to load into the barrel

87
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:12,560
than others.

88
00:07:12,561 --> 00:07:17,440
They're also possible to standardize in terms of weight.

89
00:07:17,441 --> 00:07:19,900
is a commonly occurring metal found around the world.

90
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,120
Its low melting point makes casting lead balls

91
00:07:23,121 --> 00:07:25,120
an uncomplicated low-tech process.

92
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:28,920
Using the simple lead ball, however, was complicated,

93
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:31,140
slow, and unreliable.

94
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:34,860
The gunner had to pour black powder into the smooth metal barrel

95
00:07:34,861 --> 00:07:36,840
and ram the ball into place.

96
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:42,500
Next, he had to prime the flash pan with a small amount of powder.

97
00:07:42,501 --> 00:07:50,480
after all this when he pulled the trigger the gun didn't always fire

98
00:07:50,481 --> 00:07:56,400
because the powder didn't always burn if it got wet it was totally useless and it was hard to

99
00:07:56,401 --> 00:08:03,100
keep it dry napoleonic era armies rarely fought uh running battles in the rain another even more

100
00:08:03,101 --> 00:08:10,320
serious problem was fouling the buildup of residue in the smooth metal barrel burning gunpowder

101
00:08:10,321 --> 00:08:13,660
and lead from round balls left heavy layers in the barrel,

102
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:15,840
quickly rendering the gun unusable.

103
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:21,580
And in a time of battle where you can be expected to fire 40, 50 rounds

104
00:08:21,581 --> 00:08:24,840
during one day's engagement,

105
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:29,200
after 10 rounds your gun may become completely useless to you

106
00:08:29,201 --> 00:08:32,320
if you're not able to clean out that burned up fountain.

107
00:08:32,321 --> 00:08:34,220
With nothing better to replace it,

108
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,680
the round lead ball ruled for five centuries.

109
00:08:38,681 --> 00:08:47,860
Then in the 1800s, over a period of less than 50 years, four separate discoveries would come together to create vastly more effective bullets.

110
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:55,780
The word frangible comes from the Latin word frangere, which means to break.

111
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,260
Bullets will return on Modern Marvels.

112
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,500
We now return to Bullets on Modern Marvels.

113
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:19,180
The power and amazing versatility of today's bullets depend on four key developments, all

114
00:09:19,181 --> 00:09:23,680
of them occurring in the 19th century.

115
00:09:23,681 --> 00:09:30,780
The rifled gun barrel, the conical bullet, the self-contained cartridge, and refined

116
00:09:30,781 --> 00:09:31,780
gunpowder.

117
00:09:31,781 --> 00:09:37,100
The first step in revolutionizing bullet design was the introduction of the rifled barrel.

118
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:46,220
Rifling is the spiral grooved pattern cut into the inside wall of a gun barrel.

119
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:54,300
As the bullet speeds along the barrel, the rifling sets it to spinning.

120
00:09:54,940 --> 00:10:01,640
We believe that rifled arms, though, were initially considered for an entirely different reason than they're used today.

121
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:06,680
The initial reason for rifling was probably to control the buildup of powder and lead residue,

122
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,140
which resulted in fouling the barrel.

123
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:13,540
The grooves held some of the debris, leaving the barrel a little cleaner.

124
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:19,620
There was a theory that some gunsmith cut spiral grooves in a barrel

125
00:10:19,621 --> 00:10:23,080
just to counteract the effects of powder fouling

126
00:10:23,081 --> 00:10:28,020
and found by accident that it made the bullet spin, the ball spin,

127
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:30,400
and that it was far more accurate.

128
00:10:30,401 --> 00:10:36,500
But without a grooved barrel, without a rifled barrel, we would have no accuracy at all.

129
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:40,980
In fact, rifling had little effect on the buildup of residue.

130
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:51,640
The spin, however, increased the accuracy of the ball, but only slightly, particularly when wrapped in a cloth patch.

131
00:10:51,641 --> 00:11:01,060
It would be another 200 years before the development of an entirely new kind of bullet

132
00:11:01,061 --> 00:11:03,780
made truly effective use of rifling.

133
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:11,780
In the 1840s, a French ordnance officer named Claude Etienne Minet

134
00:11:11,781 --> 00:11:19,220
perfected a crude-looking bullet with a lead slug that had a hollow base.

135
00:11:19,221 --> 00:11:22,980
because his bullet could be made undersized,

136
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:28,600
it could be rammed down even a fouled barrel very quickly with no problems.

137
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,380
But because the base was hollow,

138
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:34,960
when the pressure of the expanding powder gas hit it,

139
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:39,500
it would expand to take the rifling and spin accurately.

140
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:43,380
And it was accurate even by today's standards.

141
00:11:43,381 --> 00:11:46,080
It was a huge, huge step forward.

142
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:49,020
You can think of it much as a quarterback throwing a pass.

143
00:11:49,220 --> 00:12:01,726
If he puts a nice spiral on it, the ball will go where he's intending it to go, and it will go a longer distance than if he somehow throws the ball where it's tumbling

144
00:12:01,727 --> 00:12:02,700
end over end.

145
00:12:03,660 --> 00:12:05,780
Today we call it the mini ball.

146
00:12:05,781 --> 00:12:15,892
A little corruption of the French pronunciation, but a conical-shaped bullet's going to fly through the air better than a round ball because a bullet is piercing the

147
00:12:15,893 --> 00:12:19,140
air instead of presenting a large face of resistance.

148
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:21,000
So its friction is less.

149
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:32,870
By 1861, as America moved toward civil war, a deadly accurate long-range weapon with a fast-loading bullet was in the hands of the infantry for the first time in

150
00:12:32,871 --> 00:12:33,571
history.

151
00:12:35,780 --> 00:12:42,780
commanders failed to grasp the implications with catastrophic results

152
00:12:42,781 --> 00:12:47,820
the generals on both sides were indoctrinated with the tactics of

153
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:53,780
Napoleon now what Napoleon said you must do is line up all your troops in a row

154
00:12:53,781 --> 00:12:59,780
cheek by jowl and have a march in order toward the enemy drums and bugles blaring

155
00:12:59,781 --> 00:13:04,780
fire one volley then fix your bayonets and charge

156
00:13:04,781 --> 00:13:14,400
That dissolved very quickly when you can sit back behind a stone wall and shoot at somebody 500 yards away and knock them down.

157
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:18,240
And they won't even hear the shot fired at them before the round hits them.

158
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:25,980
Casualties on both sides were enormous because of the rifled barrel and the mini ball.

159
00:13:26,820 --> 00:13:34,000
Perhaps the single worst slaughter occurred on July the 3rd, 1863, just outside a village called Gettysburg.

160
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:48,780
In an assault led by General George Pickett, 13,500 Confederates marched in textbook formation

161
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:53,140
directly into the blazing guns of entrenched Union forces.

162
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:58,880
In about an hour, 10,000 Confederates were killed, wounded, or captured.

163
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:13,440
The miniball fired from a rifled barrel helped destroy the Confederate wave and arguably helped

164
00:14:13,441 --> 00:14:16,300
save the Union.

165
00:14:16,301 --> 00:14:21,540
Shortly before the rifled gun and the miniball proved their combined value, gun makers including

166
00:14:21,541 --> 00:14:26,900
Samuel Colt developed fixed ammunition for pistols, another of the four key developments.

167
00:14:26,901 --> 00:14:32,180
The first metallic cartridge was the pinfire, invented in France in 1828.

168
00:14:32,181 --> 00:14:35,080
A brass tube ran through the side to a percussion cap,

169
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:38,400
which ignited when hit by the hammer of a pinfire revolver.

170
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:40,920
The invention of the metallic cartridge,

171
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:45,440
in this country we have the partnership of Smith and Wesson to think.

172
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:51,260
I call it the three Ps, the primer, the propellant, and the projectile, all in one.

173
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:58,620
The three basic elements that you need to make a gun go, bang, and work are all self-contained.

174
00:14:58,621 --> 00:15:02,360
At first, self-contained ammunition was slow to catch on.

175
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:06,900
The weapons firing them were too few and too costly.

176
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:13,340
But as weapons became available, metallic cartridges modernized the world of bullets.

177
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:19,200
The impact of cartridge firing guns really came after the Civil War,

178
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:26,760
with the appearance of firearms like the Colt Peacemaker and the Winchester Model 73.

179
00:15:26,761 --> 00:15:34,360
These were truly modern firearms. Reliable, simple, fast firing and accurate.

180
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:40,060
That's when it came in from about 1873 onward.

181
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:46,660
In the late 1800s came the fourth and last innovation leading to the creation of modern bullets.

182
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:55,480
The most important invention in the history of the bullet would have to be smokeless powder.

183
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:01,160
Without smokeless powder, we would have nowhere near the velocity we have today.

184
00:16:01,300 --> 00:16:04,940
We would have nowhere near the rapid fire potential.

185
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:07,100
Everything would be different.

186
00:16:12,700 --> 00:16:17,740
Smokeless powder was more stable and predictable than black powder, and far more powerful.

187
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:23,880
The first smokeless propellants of the 1880s were chemically very similar to what we have today,

188
00:16:23,881 --> 00:16:26,140
being primarily nitrocellulose compounds,

189
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:29,920
a mixture of cellulose, nitroglycerin, and certain nitrates

190
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:33,980
that made it possible to come up with smaller, elongated, jacketed bullets

191
00:16:33,981 --> 00:16:35,240
driven at very high velocity.

192
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:43,220
A mini-ball could be fired at maybe 1,200 feet per second with black powder.

193
00:16:43,660 --> 00:16:49,560
With smokeless powder, you could get a smaller slug moving at literally twice that velocity.

194
00:16:49,561 --> 00:17:03,020
The combination of the rifled barrel, the conical projectile, smokeless powder, and self-contained ammunition gave bullets unprecedented accuracy and power.

195
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:06,280
The bullet continued to change to meet new demands.

196
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:16,800
Late in the 19th century, Hiram Maxim's rapid-firing belt-fed machine gun worked best with a bullet tailored for it, pointed and more slender.

197
00:17:16,801 --> 00:17:22,920
Now we can fire guns at rates of 600 and 800 rounds a minute,

198
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:25,720
where before the Civil War it was three rounds a minute.

199
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:32,520
The murderous efficiency of the machine gun and the new bullet

200
00:17:32,521 --> 00:17:35,080
was proven on the battlefields of World War I.

201
00:17:35,960 --> 00:17:39,380
In a deadly escalation, the British developed the tank

202
00:17:39,381 --> 00:17:41,520
to protect their troops from machine gun fire.

203
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:45,680
To defeat the tank, the Germans created the first armor-piercing bullet.

204
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:51,400
The German J-Round, a 13-millimeter or .50 caliber armor-piercing bullet,

205
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:54,220
gave them a chance against behemoths of steel.

206
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:59,420
You could have two men armed with this gigantic rifle

207
00:17:59,421 --> 00:18:02,600
firing armored piercing rounds at British tanks

208
00:18:02,601 --> 00:18:05,240
and put them right out of commission fairly quickly.

209
00:18:06,140 --> 00:18:09,960
The armor-piercing rounds were created by inserting a solid core of steel

210
00:18:09,961 --> 00:18:12,200
into the center of conventional bullets

211
00:18:12,201 --> 00:18:14,800
to keep them from crumpling when they hit a tank.

212
00:18:14,801 --> 00:18:20,360
In a later development, this corps was reinforced with a nickel-cadmium plating.

213
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:26,920
Even the most devastating bullets are useless if they don't hit their target.

214
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:29,560
To help gunners see where their rounds were going,

215
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:34,080
Army Ordnance Officers looked back in history to the Civil War for answers.

216
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:39,360
Being able to visually identify where your projectile is in flight

217
00:18:39,361 --> 00:18:44,880
easily have developed from lubricant used on various projectiles of the black powder range.

218
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:49,680
Sometimes they would leave a little smoke trail as the bullets went downrange.

219
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:52,480
Smokeless powder had eliminated the trail,

220
00:18:52,481 --> 00:18:57,220
so the tracer bullet was created by attaching a pellet made of magnesium and phosphate

221
00:18:57,221 --> 00:18:59,560
to the base of an ordinary bullet.

222
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:04,200
When the bullet is fired, it is clearly visible.

223
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:07,080
Usually every fifth round is a tracer.

224
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:13,900
Military bullets have undergone numerous refinements over the years,

225
00:19:13,901 --> 00:19:18,040
but their basic design has remained much the same since World War I.

226
00:19:18,540 --> 00:19:22,780
Lead cores wrapped with copper, known as the full metal jacket.

227
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:27,960
Most bullets today are far better than they were 20 years ago,

228
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:30,160
much better than they were 10 years ago.

229
00:19:31,060 --> 00:19:33,400
While some are deadlier than their predecessors,

230
00:19:34,460 --> 00:19:38,660
others actually offer the chance to save a life rather than take one in a shooting.

231
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:43,240
The challenge is determining which to use in what situation.

232
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:51,040
The single-shot muzzle-loading rifle caused 80% of all wounds during the Civil War.

233
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:54,160
Its primary bullet was the conical-shaped mini-ball.

234
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:57,140
Bullets will return on Modern Marvels.

235
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:06,180
Bullets today come in hundreds of different sizes, shapes, and weights.

236
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:13,520
Determining which works best depends entirely on what the shooter wants the bullet to do when it hits the target.

237
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,600
The target is everything.

238
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:19,860
The bullet is designed for the target.

239
00:20:20,420 --> 00:20:26,500
You can shoot holes in paper, or you can shoot holes in quarter-inch mild steel armor plating.

240
00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:30,540
You can hunt elephants, or you can hunt squirrels.

241
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:34,320
And you can't do all of that with the same cartridge.

242
00:20:36,180 --> 00:20:39,600
Police need to be able to stop a dangerous suspect.

243
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:43,120
Soldiers need a bullet that will penetrate an enemy target.

244
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:48,300
Hunters want to kill their prey as quickly as possible.

245
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:54,560
As an example, the .22 short that's fired out of this rapid-fire pistol made by Benelli

246
00:20:54,561 --> 00:20:59,300
is probably only powerful enough to punch a hole in a piece of cardboard on an Olympic target.

247
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:04,780
On the other hand, the .45 Long Colt, with its 250-grain solid lead bullet,

248
00:21:04,781 --> 00:21:07,840
as fired out of this Smith & Wesson Model 25 revolver

249
00:21:07,841 --> 00:21:10,960
is certainly powerful enough to stop a human being.

250
00:21:12,260 --> 00:21:15,240
In the world of bullets, size matters.

251
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:19,700
Size is the diameter as expressed in caliber.

252
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:23,640
When we're talking about caliber, what we're really discussing

253
00:21:23,641 --> 00:21:26,320
is a measurement of the diameter of the bullet itself,

254
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:29,360
usually described either in millimeters or decimal inch.

255
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:33,620
American ammunition makers usually measure diameter

256
00:21:33,621 --> 00:21:35,000
in fractions of an inch.

257
00:21:36,740 --> 00:21:41,000
A .22 caliber bullet, commonly referred to simply as a .22,

258
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:44,880
measures .22 one-hundredths of an inch in diameter.

259
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:49,760
The U.S. military usually calculates caliber in millimeters

260
00:21:49,761 --> 00:21:51,480
rather than fractions of an inch.

261
00:21:53,060 --> 00:21:55,660
Thus, their long version of the .22 caliber round

262
00:21:55,661 --> 00:21:59,460
is designated as a 5.56 millimeter.

263
00:22:00,280 --> 00:22:03,720
The long and the regular .22 round have the same size bullet.

264
00:22:04,440 --> 00:22:06,120
It's the casing size that varies.

265
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:15,340
For example, the .45 Long Colt caliber cartridge has a bullet diameter of .451 or .452 in its modern loading,

266
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:22,940
which is the same as the .45 ACP cartridge as loaded for the old .45 government model automatic pistol.

267
00:22:23,460 --> 00:22:26,460
As you can see, they're two completely different cartridges, but the same caliber.

268
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:30,480
The longer the cartridge, the more powder it holds.

269
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:34,320
The more powder it holds, the faster you can drive the bullet.

270
00:22:34,321 --> 00:22:38,320
The faster you can drive the bullet, the more energy it builds up.

271
00:22:40,340 --> 00:22:45,680
The basic rule is, the bigger the target, the bigger the bullet needed for optimal effect.

272
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:52,300
Less powerful bullets with short cartridges, such as the .22 and the .25,

273
00:22:52,301 --> 00:22:56,500
are used mostly for target practice.

274
00:22:59,620 --> 00:23:04,440
Police and military prefer medium caliber pistol bullets, such as the .45,

275
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:09,000
powerful enough to stop an aggressor without continuing through to hit bystanders.

276
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:13,280
The larger the round, the greater the amount of powder propelling the bullet.

277
00:23:15,620 --> 00:23:21,320
Military and police snipers typically use a .308, or larger caliber, rifle bullet

278
00:23:21,321 --> 00:23:23,920
to achieve instant disabling of the subject.

279
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:31,100
The round's higher velocity also makes it more accurate at great distances.

280
00:23:35,320 --> 00:23:38,140
The size and power of the bullet aren't the only factors

281
00:23:38,141 --> 00:23:40,260
that affect what happens in that critical moment

282
00:23:40,261 --> 00:23:41,960
when the bullet hits the target.

283
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:50,980
The bullet's shape, as well as the materials used to make it,

284
00:23:50,981 --> 00:23:52,980
affect its performance.

285
00:23:54,980 --> 00:23:58,980
If you are going for a long range, you need to make the bullet streamlined.

286
00:23:58,981 --> 00:24:02,980
If you're going for maximum tissue damage at short range,

287
00:24:02,981 --> 00:24:06,980
you can have the bullet be blunt or hollow.

288
00:24:06,981 --> 00:24:10,980
On impact, the jacket of a hollow point instantly peels back,

289
00:24:10,981 --> 00:24:14,980
exposing the soft lead core, which then expands against the target.

290
00:24:20,980 --> 00:24:25,980
Modern bullets use a hollow point design which is designed to mushroom.

291
00:24:25,981 --> 00:24:30,980
And what this does is it makes the front surface area of the bullet much larger,

292
00:24:30,981 --> 00:24:34,980
transfers much more of the muzzle energy to the suspect.

293
00:24:34,981 --> 00:24:38,980
A solid-nosed bullet penetrates a soft target more easily

294
00:24:38,981 --> 00:24:44,980
because of the hard copper and zinc alloy wrapped around the soft lead core.

295
00:24:44,981 --> 00:24:49,980
Used by NATO military forces, this is the bullet known as the full metal jacket.

296
00:24:49,981 --> 00:24:58,280
We're basically restricted to the standard ball round.

297
00:24:58,281 --> 00:25:03,860
Industry offers expanding bullets, flattening bullets, mushrooming bullets, but law of war,

298
00:25:03,861 --> 00:25:10,080
the Hague Convention restricts us to rounds that will not induce undue suffering to the

299
00:25:10,081 --> 00:25:12,080
combatant.

300
00:25:12,081 --> 00:25:19,880
In recent years, because of environmental concerns about lead, the U.S. military has

301
00:25:19,881 --> 00:25:23,040
has changed the lead cores used in its bullets.

302
00:25:23,041 --> 00:25:27,480
Hundreds of live fire training sites and civilian firing ranges across the country

303
00:25:27,481 --> 00:25:30,440
have been closed because of lead contamination.

304
00:25:30,441 --> 00:25:34,260
Lead causes birth defects in children.

305
00:25:34,261 --> 00:25:36,980
It can cause memory loss.

306
00:25:36,981 --> 00:25:41,180
Lead has a bunch of very negative environmental aspects to it.

307
00:25:41,181 --> 00:25:45,840
It's an environmental issue, and it's one of our major concerns,

308
00:25:45,841 --> 00:25:50,520
is cleaning up the ranges that have been contaminated with lead.

309
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,180
And one of the efforts the Marine Corps has taken right now

310
00:25:53,181 --> 00:25:56,560
is to overhaul our small caliber ammunition

311
00:25:56,561 --> 00:26:03,220
and transition from lead to a tungsten nylon projectile.

312
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:07,120
The lead-free replacement is called the green bullet,

313
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:09,000
which is also a fragile.

314
00:26:09,940 --> 00:26:12,700
Tests confirm that while it is environmentally friendly,

315
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:15,140
it's just as lethal on the battlefield.

316
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:20,880
And that was one of the things that we made sure that we were going to do before we fielded a green round,

317
00:26:20,980 --> 00:26:25,320
is to make sure that we were getting the same product minus the lead.

318
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:27,120
For all U.S.

319
00:26:27,121 --> 00:26:32,580
forces, replacement of lead core bullets with those made with tungsten, tin, or nylon cores

320
00:26:32,581 --> 00:26:36,140
is scheduled for completion by 2008.

321
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:43,720
Since the Matrixes contain no lead, of course, you don't have the lead cleanup problem

322
00:26:43,721 --> 00:26:46,440
that was the environmental problem of the past.

323
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:50,200
In addition to the materials used to make it,

324
00:26:50,540 --> 00:26:53,580
a key factor determining the impact of any bullet on the target

325
00:26:53,581 --> 00:26:55,600
is its muzzle velocity.

326
00:26:56,480 --> 00:27:00,200
Muzzle velocity is simply the speed or the velocity

327
00:27:00,201 --> 00:27:03,620
with which the bullet leaves the muzzle of the gun.

328
00:27:04,140 --> 00:27:06,620
It's the fastest it's ever going to go.

329
00:27:07,400 --> 00:27:09,560
From that point on, it will be slowing down.

330
00:27:10,140 --> 00:27:12,580
Because of modern higher-energy powders,

331
00:27:13,320 --> 00:27:19,620
The bullet's initial velocity can be as high as 5,000 feet per second, or 3,400 miles per hour.

332
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:29,400
No matter how streamlined you make a bullet, air resistance still works on it.

333
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:31,680
Gravity affects it somewhat.

334
00:27:32,340 --> 00:27:38,460
If you held a bullet alongside the muzzle of a rifle and dropped it at the instant you fired,

335
00:27:38,461 --> 00:27:43,260
the bullet you dropped and the bullet you fired would hit the ground at the same time.

336
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:48,600
In recent years, high-speed cameras and computer models

337
00:27:48,601 --> 00:27:51,760
have greatly improved the ability to evaluate bullet performance.

338
00:27:56,460 --> 00:27:59,720
Complex equations help predict the effect of design changes.

339
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:02,860
But there are still surprises in the firing range.

340
00:28:02,861 --> 00:28:12,520
in the end you have to get out and shoot the thing a number of bullet companies have gotten

341
00:28:12,521 --> 00:28:17,660
pretty badly burned in the past because they designed something that looked great in the lab

342
00:28:17,661 --> 00:28:21,540
and looked great on paper but didn't work so well in the field

343
00:28:21,541 --> 00:28:26,900
so you need about half scientific testing and about half real world

344
00:28:26,901 --> 00:28:36,040
In the real world, failure to use the right bullet can have deadly consequences.

345
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:47,920
A competition pistol champion may shoot up to 100,000 rounds of ammunition per year.

346
00:28:50,020 --> 00:28:55,340
Bullets will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

347
00:28:56,900 --> 00:29:03,020
North Hollywood, California, February 28, 1997.

348
00:29:03,760 --> 00:29:08,200
The scene of the most violent shootout in modern American police history.

349
00:29:09,580 --> 00:29:16,260
For 44 minutes, two heavily armed bank robbers managed to hold off the best the LAPD could bring to bear.

350
00:29:17,080 --> 00:29:26,620
The robbers, using fully automatic assault rifles, unleashed a relentless barrage of high-velocity .223 and .308 armor-piercing bullets.

351
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:31,800
The police fired back with everything they had,

352
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:38,040
but their 9mm and .38 caliber bullets just bounced off the body armor worn by the criminals.

353
00:29:38,380 --> 00:29:44,040
Standard issue was a .38 revolver or a 9mm automatic and a 12-gauge shotgun,

354
00:29:44,920 --> 00:29:48,760
neither of which will come close to coping with body armor.

355
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:54,440
By the time the carnage was over, nearly 2,000 rounds had been exchanged.

356
00:29:55,320 --> 00:29:58,500
Seven civilians and 11 police officers have been wounded.

357
00:30:00,120 --> 00:30:02,020
One suspect killed himself.

358
00:30:02,760 --> 00:30:07,200
The other bled to death after an LAPD SWAT team managed to hit him in his legs,

359
00:30:07,820 --> 00:30:09,420
which were unprotected by armor.

360
00:30:10,820 --> 00:30:14,820
The North Hollywood shootout scared a lot of cops real good.

361
00:30:14,821 --> 00:30:20,380
It informed them very plainly that the weapons they were carrying on the job

362
00:30:20,381 --> 00:30:24,460
were not nearly big enough for some of the situations they had to handle.

363
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:28,760
For general police work, the solution was not to simply use a bigger bullet.

364
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:35,780
We can't use oversized bullets because it's just too much for an officer control.

365
00:30:36,200 --> 00:30:38,380
We can't use undersized bullets that we'll never miss

366
00:30:38,381 --> 00:30:41,640
because then they wouldn't be very efficient on the person we're trying to stop anyway.

367
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:45,060
The challenge we have is finding that balance between bullet design

368
00:30:45,061 --> 00:30:47,800
that's going to transfer as much energy as possible to the suspect

369
00:30:47,801 --> 00:30:51,820
and controllability for the officers so that the recoil is not so excessive

370
00:30:51,821 --> 00:30:54,280
that the gun is bouncing all over the place.

371
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:58,000
From the early 1900s until the late 1970s,

372
00:30:58,440 --> 00:31:03,000
the standard issue for police officers was the .38 caliber lead-nosed bullet.

373
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:09,140
Often it lacked the knockdown punch needed to stop criminals in their tracks.

374
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:12,240
They wouldn't do much damage,

375
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:15,920
and the person would literally be very unaffected by being shot by the round.

376
00:31:17,800 --> 00:31:27,500
The search for a more powerful bullet led to the development of a fearsome new round, the .357 Magnum.

377
00:31:31,640 --> 00:31:38,680
All .357 Magnum is, the truth be told, is simply a .38 Special made longer, and Magnum simply means larger.

378
00:31:39,820 --> 00:31:44,040
The .357 Magnum is considered, for the most part, too powerful, too much,

379
00:31:44,041 --> 00:31:49,040
And so we stuck with the .38 Special clear through the early 1970s.

380
00:31:52,080 --> 00:32:00,500
Following a tragic 1986 Miami shootout in which two FBI agents using underpowered 9mm and .38 caliber bullets were killed,

381
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:05,860
police agencies looked at the Army-issue .45 caliber Colt.

382
00:32:08,740 --> 00:32:11,700
For the average officer, that creates a lot of problems,

383
00:32:11,701 --> 00:32:14,380
of flinching, anticipating that excessive recoil,

384
00:32:14,780 --> 00:32:15,760
and they become inefficient.

385
00:32:15,900 --> 00:32:16,680
They don't shoot well.

386
00:32:19,580 --> 00:32:22,660
Then in the 1990s, police around the country

387
00:32:22,661 --> 00:32:25,680
began settling on a bullet that best satisfied their needs,

388
00:32:26,120 --> 00:32:27,620
the .40 caliber hollow point.

389
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:30,000
We have a modern hollow point round.

390
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:32,960
They can fit in a small enough weapon that people can control it.

391
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:34,420
The rounds are hitting the suspects,

392
00:32:34,421 --> 00:32:36,700
not going through the suspect and harming bystanders,

393
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:38,420
and it's very efficient.

394
00:32:38,640 --> 00:32:41,420
And even the smallest, slightest-built officers

395
00:32:41,421 --> 00:32:44,420
can control that weapon and have confidence that it's going to work for them.

396
00:32:46,560 --> 00:32:50,600
Sometimes no off-the-shelf bullet meets a user's particular needs.

397
00:32:52,700 --> 00:32:57,020
For maximum performance, thousands of shooters hand-load their ammunition.

398
00:32:57,900 --> 00:33:00,440
Among them is a group of Marines at Quantico, Virginia.

399
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:03,580
At the test facility, what we're trying to do

400
00:33:03,581 --> 00:33:08,080
is come up with the most accurate rounds, obviously matched to the weapon.

401
00:33:08,081 --> 00:33:15,080
The process begins with gunsmiths modifying standard issue weapons to meet special requirements.

402
00:33:15,081 --> 00:33:18,080
For different guns, we have different requirements.

403
00:33:18,081 --> 00:33:23,080
We can't build a sniper rifle to the same requirements as a pommel rifle.

404
00:33:23,081 --> 00:33:28,080
The pommel rifle is used for match. The sniper rifle would be used for combat.

405
00:33:28,081 --> 00:33:31,080
It's all about performance of the weapon with the ammunition.

406
00:33:38,080 --> 00:33:42,880
The elements used to build the bullets arrive from manufacturers as separate components.

407
00:33:43,020 --> 00:33:48,420
It's hard to do mass production of ammunition and keep the consistency there.

408
00:33:48,940 --> 00:33:53,960
That's why we weigh each projectile, where we weigh each casing, where we weigh each primer,

409
00:33:54,440 --> 00:33:58,700
and then we put it all together, and then we weigh it again for consistency.

410
00:33:58,980 --> 00:34:02,160
Consistency is the key to the perfect bullet.

411
00:34:03,180 --> 00:34:06,700
Each bullet is tailor-made for the specific weapon that will fire it.

412
00:34:06,701 --> 00:34:12,700
An ammunition loader turns a neck sizing brush all the way around the outside diameter

413
00:34:12,701 --> 00:34:18,460
to bring it down to a desired thickness for holding the projectile or bullet with uniform tension.

414
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:23,680
He then trims the length to ensure it fits into the assigned rifle chamber properly,

415
00:34:24,720 --> 00:34:28,840
measuring it for consistent length for even release of the projectile or bullet.

416
00:34:28,841 --> 00:34:36,940
Another ammunition loader transfers a tube filled with explosive primers to a hand operated

417
00:34:36,941 --> 00:34:38,315
priming tool.

418
00:34:38,316 --> 00:34:46,140
Then he individually inserts each primer in the base of the brass casings.

419
00:34:46,141 --> 00:34:51,345
The primer is the actual initiator for the powder charge that comes later.

420
00:34:51,346 --> 00:34:52,400
The next loader

421
00:34:52,401 --> 00:34:55,534
weighs each bullet in increments of a tenth of a grain.

422
00:34:55,535 --> 00:34:57,300
In this case the correct weight

423
00:34:57,301 --> 00:35:01,420
is exactly one hundred forty two point zero grains

424
00:35:01,421 --> 00:35:03,620
a third of an ounce

425
00:35:03,621 --> 00:35:09,020
these are set off to the side until the casing is charged with gunpowder

426
00:35:09,021 --> 00:35:12,800
marine handling the propellant or gunpowder measures out the exact amount

427
00:35:12,801 --> 00:35:18,800
for each load

428
00:35:18,801 --> 00:35:25,800
and then carefully pours it into place

429
00:35:25,801 --> 00:35:30,800
With firm steady pressure, he finishes the round by fitting the bullet into the filled casing.

430
00:35:31,800 --> 00:35:36,800
His final measurement confirms that the round's overall length is precisely correct.

431
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:43,800
Then we package up the rounds with the load name that we assign it, the date that we produced it,

432
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:50,800
the gun that that ammo is actually for, and the quantity that's within that box.

433
00:35:50,801 --> 00:35:59,340
Laboratory testing of random samples checks chamber pressure, velocity, and other performance

434
00:35:59,341 --> 00:36:01,300
characteristics.

435
00:36:01,301 --> 00:36:08,760
To simulate extreme weather conditions, some bullets are heated to 120 plus degrees.

436
00:36:08,761 --> 00:36:12,020
Others are cooled to minus 25.

437
00:36:12,021 --> 00:36:18,040
The ultimate goal is to give the shooter on the line the best possible weapon and the

438
00:36:18,041 --> 00:36:21,160
the best possible ammunition married up to that weapon.

439
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:26,880
We're trying to take all the errors that we can out of the process.

440
00:36:29,600 --> 00:36:33,440
Final performance testing of the ammunition takes place on the firing line.

441
00:36:34,240 --> 00:36:38,320
The precision shooters conducting the trials are the Marine Corps' best,

442
00:36:38,680 --> 00:36:42,000
snipers and members of the Corps' national competition teams.

443
00:36:42,001 --> 00:36:50,100
Once the testing is complete through precision weapons,

444
00:36:50,640 --> 00:36:54,000
they will forward the results of those tests back to the manufacturer

445
00:36:54,001 --> 00:36:57,380
to where components will be made to that spec.

446
00:36:58,640 --> 00:37:00,860
Whether hand-loaded or manufactured,

447
00:37:01,540 --> 00:37:03,740
most ammunition today is highly effective.

448
00:37:04,280 --> 00:37:06,700
But experts are on the verge of creating bullets

449
00:37:06,701 --> 00:37:08,720
that seem to think for themselves.

450
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:21,960
According to the FBI, of the 56 law enforcement officers feloniously killed in the line of duty in 2002,

451
00:37:22,760 --> 00:37:27,500
38 died from bullets fired from handguns and 9 from rifle bullets.

452
00:37:28,300 --> 00:37:30,220
Bullets will return on Modern Marvels.

453
00:37:31,880 --> 00:37:36,840
Police and military would like a bullet able to punch through body armor and bring down the target,

454
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:40,720
but not pass all the way through and hit an innocent bystander.

455
00:37:40,721 --> 00:37:45,680
Such a bullet seemed impossible until recently.

456
00:37:46,680 --> 00:37:54,180
We offer in a single round the ability to defeat hard and soft body armor barriers

457
00:37:54,181 --> 00:38:02,800
and offer performance that only has been accomplished by frangible ammunition prior to our bullets.

458
00:38:02,801 --> 00:38:15,420
To create the new ammunition, eight metals are bonded together through a unique proprietary method.

459
00:38:16,520 --> 00:38:20,260
The difference between a conventional bullet and the new one is dramatic.

